  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Lobito Corridor: An opportunity for Women in the Mining Sector in the DRC.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Lobito Corridor is a major railway and logistics project linking the Port of Lobito in Angola to the mining regions of Zambia and the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), via Kolwezi and Lubumbashi. Supported by a partnership between the Angolan, Zambian, and Congolese governments, as well as by the United States and the European Union, this corridor serves as a strategic link for regional trade and the global energy transition.

In a context where infrastructure projects are attracting international investment, it is crucial to train women, strengthen their capacity to access regional markets, and structure cross-border cooperation in the Angola-DRC-Zambia region. 

By providing an alternative export route to the Atlantic Ocean, the Lobito Corridor is expected to reduce transportation costs, enhance the competitiveness of the region&#x2019;s minerals, and stimulate economic development along its route. However, women&#x2014;who make up a significant portion of the workforce in mining communities and local supply chains&#x2014;risk being left on the sidelines if inclusion measures are not integrated from the outset in the design and implementation of projects linked to this corridor. 

The project aimed to strengthen technical, entrepreneurial, and institutional capacities of women operating in mining subcontracting. Propose concrete action plans to promote the integration of women into projects related to the Lobito Corridor.</narrative>
  </description>
